{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The nocturnal UHI gap is an artifact of different cooling rates driven by surface emissivity and aridity: rural surfaces with high emissivity and adequate moisture cool rapidly after sunset, whereas urban surfaces exhibit lower effective emissivity, causing the city to retain heat longer and cool more slowly.","counterconditions":["If measured urban surface emissivity is found to be systematically higher than surrounding rural surfaces, this mechanism is overturned.","If the nocturnal UHI gap is empirically observed to be larger in humid climates than in arid climates due to urban irrigation creating a localized moisture surplus, this mechanism is refuted.","If surface energy balance measurements show rural surfaces in arid regions cool slower than urban surfaces at night, this mechanism fails.","If the cooling rate differential is entirely explained by anthropogenic heat release independent of surface material properties or background aridity, this mechanism is superseded."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"After sunset, net longwave radiative loss dominates the surface energy balance. The cooling rate of a surface is directly governed by its thermal admittance and its ability to radiate heat to the sky. In arid or semi-arid regions, rural surfaces (e.g., dry sand, bare soil, rock) often have high emissivity and very low thermal inertia, allowing them to cool rapidly. In contrast, urban materials often have lower emissivity and higher thermal mass, slowing their nocturnal cooling. The cross-city modulator is aridity: cities in arid climates are surrounded by rural areas that cool exceptionally fast due to lack of moisture (no latent heat buffer) and unobstructed radiative loss, artificially inflating the urban-rural temperature gap compared to humid cities where rural cooling is buffered by evapotranspiration.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"search_signal":"need_context"},"typicality":0.3}],"simulation_proposals":[]}